<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nush-i Jan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tepe Nush-i Jan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nush-i Jan (Tepe Nush-i Jan) is an archaeological site in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, near Malayer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a> Province, excavated by a British team under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Stronach from 1967. It preserves a fortified Median complex of the eighth and seventh centuries BCE with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fire-temple">fire temple</a>, a fort and a columned hall. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godin-tepe">Godin Tepe</a> it is one of the main sources for Median architecture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nush-i-jan.jpg" alt="Nush-i Jan" width="320" height="204" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shinse110 at Persian Wikipedianoshijan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abdoli.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
